    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let's dive into a technical example. Suppose you have an Employees table with the following columns:\n\n| Employee ID | Name | Department | Manager ID | Salary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | John | Sales | 2 | 50000 |\n| 2 | Jane | Marketing | 3 | 60000 |\n| 3 | Bob | IT | NULL | 70000 |\n\nIn this example, the Department attribute depends on the Manager ID attribute. This is a transitive dependency because the Department attribute is dependent on the Manager ID attribute, which in turn is dependent on the Employee ID attribute (since the Manager ID is a foreign key referencing the Employee ID).\n\nThink of it like a goblin's family tree - if you know the Manager ID, you can infer the Department, but if you only know the Employee ID, you can't determine the Department without knowing the Manager ID. This is a transitive dependency, and it's not normalized.\n\nTo normalize this table to 3NF, you would need to create a new table, say, Departments, with the Department ID as a primary key and the Department name as an attribute. Then, you would create another table, say, Employee",
